y  poster child for the saying with the  proper execution there's nothing that's  too wild or crazy to work and i guess  that statement stands given the audience  reception for the show and it's modern  sequels  i intentionally placed baki and gengar  are still right next to each other  because honestly speaking the two do  have a lot in similar both are fighting  anime with journey arcs both have the  same vibes in terms of fights and both  are as brutal and intense as each other  heck i wouldn't be surprised if gengar  ashura ended up being inspired by baki  people have criticized gengar ashura for  the cg but i personally don't think it  detracts from it too much i mean it's  not berserk tear or god forbid ari  firethat i think that despite the cg the  anime still manages to capture the real  important things for a fighting anime  the emotional impact of the actual  fights and the characters who all come  in fighting for something he had a  somewhat slow start but picks up pretty  quickly and while the plot does seem  straightforward i myself thoroughly  enjoyed it it's available on netflix if  you're up for some viciously passionate  fights  i want to end this list with something  most of you are familiar with and i  think the best mainstream anime for it  would be naruto  naruto is one of the shows that spawned  an entire generation of fans i know i  know hand-to-hand combat's not the first  thing you think of when we say naruto  however in that batch of mainstream  shonen that took over in the 2000s is  the one that emphasized combat  specifically had to hand combat the most  well you just got to look at how awesome  rockley's major fights were despite  largely being comic relief he and guy  have some of the most memorable fights  that made our childhood and of course  who could forget that gargashi vs obito  fight naruto's a show featuring ninjas  that barely act like actual ninjas and  come equipped with demon beasts magical  eyes and the like but all that c